                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="758-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ></span>Studio Projects TB1</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Cardioid Tube Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The TB1 is an inexpensive fixed-cardioid tube microphone manufactured by 797 Audio in China, to Studio Projects&#8217; specifications. </p><p>The capsule, as in the B1 FET, is a 34mm design with a single ~25mm, 3-micron diaphragm.</p><p>The tube amplifier circuit is relatively simple, lacking a pad, high-pass filter or pattern selection control. The tube is a 6072 dual-triode. A transformer is used for impedance conversion.</p><p>The TB1 includes an elastic shockmount, a dedicated power supply (110/220V), and a 7-pin power cable. It ships in an aluminum flight case.</p><p><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/studioprojects/tb1_orig.gif" width="80" alt="Studio Projects TB1, original branding, circa 2004" class="thumb shaft" />Like most of Studio Projects&#8217; mics, the TB1 was redesigned in 2006; the published specs show a 2dB improvement in sensitivity. Apart from that, the branding changed (the 797 Audio logo was removed) and the retail price changed (which dropped from 2006 MSRPs of $389-$479 to $299.99).</p><blockquote><p><cite>MIX</cite></p><p>side-by-side male vocal recordings through the TB1 and a $2,000 <a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/Rode/Classic-ll">RODE Classic II</a> &#8212; an undeniably great mic &#8212; were a real eye-opener. The RODE definitely sounded bigger (and in fact, it is bigger!) and is more midrange-forward, but I couldn&#8217;t say that the TB1 sounded worse in this application &#8212; just different.</p><p>Normally, you can identify inexpensive mics right away by their steely high end, but the TB1&#8217;s high end is smooth and open, toe-to-toe with the RODE &#8212; very impressive.</p></blockquote><p></p><blockquote><p><cite>DigitalProSound</cite></p><p>The TB1 exhibited a kind of silkiness that smoothed out some of the rough edges of my voice; it got better and better the more I experimented. I brought Glenda back in to sing a little and though she sounded perfect to me through the <a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/Studio-Projects/C1">C1</a>, I would have picked the TB1 in a comparative shootout. Her voice is already silky smooth, and this mic just took it to the next level.</p></blockquote><p><p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Studio-Projects/TB1">Studio Projects TB1</a> is also known as: TB-1.</p>
